{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Equation.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Equation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"equation equation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Equation.md","fileName":"Equation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Equation","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Linear Equations and Modeling]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Linear Equations and Modeling","Algebraic Identity","Conditional Equation","Equivalent Equations","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Equation.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Equation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"equation equation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Equation.md","fileName":"Equation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Equation","heading":"Equation","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An equation is an assertion that two expressions have equal value. The equals sign records a relationship, not an instruction to calculate only one side.\n\nAn [[Algebraic Identity]] is true for every permitted value of its variables, while a [[Conditional Equation]] is true only for particular values. Solving a conditional equation finds those values by producing [[Equivalent Equations]]. The balance must be preserved: an operation applied to one side must be applied compatibly to the other, and any division must respect nonzero restrictions.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Linear Equations and Modeling","Algebraic Identity","Conditional Equation","Equivalent Equations","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Equation.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Equation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"equation equation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/